{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":6,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_1306202651#3_2673088012","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":4,"snippet":"very harsh conditions, particularly where solar power is not feasible. The importance of such power sources was illustrated by the European Space Agency's Rosetta mission, which successfully landed the Philae probe on comet 67P/Churymov–Gerasimenko in 2014. Equipped with batteries and solar panels, the position in which Philae came to rest on the comet's surface – shielded from the Sun's rays by cliffs – meant that the lander was unable to make use of solar energy and was only able to send 64 hours' worth of data before its battery power ran out. So far over 45 RTGs have powered in excess of 25 US space vehicles including Apollo, Pioneer, Viking, Voyager, Galileo, Ulysses, Cassini and New Horizons space missions as well as many civil and military satellites. The Cassini <cover>spacecraft carried three RTGs providing 870 watts of power from 33 kg plutonium-238 </cover>oxide as it explored Saturn.\" , \"start_char\": 2661.000000 , \"title\": \"Nuclear Reactors for Space - World Nuclear Association\" , \"url\": \"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/non-power-nuclear-applications/transport/nuclear-reactors-for-space.aspx\" }"},"time":17},"model_calls":6,"document_judgments":4,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
