{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":29,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ mox \"mixed oxide\") (+ reactor reactors fuel electricity))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ mox \"mixed oxide\") (+ reactor reactors fuel electricity))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#6_3696778256","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q973","rank":1,"snippet":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#6_3696778256\" , \"end_char\": 6126.000000 , \"headings\": \"MOX fuel MOX fuel Contents Overview Current applications Thermal reactors Fast reactors Fabrication Dry mixing Coprecipitation Americium content Curium content Thorium MOX See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"AECL reported to the United States National Academy of Sciences committee on plutonium disposition that it has extensive experience in testing the use of MOX fuel containing from 0.5 to 3% plutonium. The content of un-burnt <cover>plutonium in spent MOX fuel </cover>from thermal reactors is significant – greater than 50% of the initial plutonium loading. However, during the burning of MOX the ratio of fissile (odd numbered) isotopes to non-fissile (even) drops from around 65% to 20%, depending on burn up. This makes any attempt to recover the fissile isotopes difficult and any bulk Pu recovered would require such a high fraction of Pu in any second generation MOX that it would be impractical. This means that such a spent fuel would be difficult to reprocess for further reuse (burning) of plutonium. Regular reprocessing of biphasic spent MOX is "},"time":999},"model_calls":29,"document_judgments":25,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
