{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":30,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q973"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q973"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#10_3696785392","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q973","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#10_3696785392\" , \"end_char\": 8564.000000 , \"headings\": \"MOX fuel MOX fuel Contents Overview Current applications Thermal reactors Fast reactors Fabrication Dry mixing Coprecipitation Americium content Curium content Thorium MOX See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"Of the total nuclear fuel used today, MOX provides 2%. Licensing and safety issues of using <cover>MOX fuel include: As plutonium </cover>isotopes absorb more neutrons than uranium fuels, reactor control systems may need modification. MOX fuel tends to run hotter because of lower thermal conductivity, which may be an issue in some reactor designs. Fission gas release in MOX fuel assemblies may limit the maximum burn-up time of MOX fuel. About 30% of the plutonium originally loaded into MOX fuel is consumed by use in a thermal reactor. In theory, if one third of the core fuel load is MOX and two-thirds uranium fuel, there is zero net gain of plutonium in the spent fuel, and the cycle could be repeated; however, there remains multiple difficulties in reprocessing spent MOX fuel. As of "},"time":8},"model_calls":30,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
