{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q979","rank":5,"snippet":"Marco_Romero \" , \"segment\": \"INL also assembles, tests, and delivers the power source to NASA. More Info WHAT IS AN MMRTG, AND HOW DOES IT WORK? Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators are ideal for space missions because they are compact, durable and reliable, providing continuous power over long periods of time. The Department of Energy provides <cover>radioisotope power systems to NASA for civil space applications. The MMRTG for the Mars 2020 mission was fueled and tested at the DOE’s Idaho National Laboratory. This spring it was shipped directly from INL to the launch site at Kennedy Space Center Florida for integration into the rover. MMRTGs work by converting heat from the natural decay of radioisotope materials into electricity. The generators consist of two major elements: a heat source that contains plutonium-238 (</cover>Pu-238) and thermocouples that convert the plutonium’s decay heat energy and the cold of space to electricity. The MMRTG is designed to produce about 110 watts of electrical power to begin the mission.\" , \"start_char\": 4120.000000 , \"title\": \"Mars 2020 | Powering NASA’s Mars Perseverance Rover - INL\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":21,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":21,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_664873627#0_1353834332","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q979","rank":6,"snippet":"And even though they’re 18 billion km (11 billion miles) from the Sun, they’re still valuable scientifically. But they’re running out of energy, and if NASA wants them to continue on much longer, they have some decisions to make. The Energy Problem The energy issue is becoming more and more critical over time for the Voyagers. Not only do their scientific instruments require energy, but the spacecraft need to keep themselves warm in the frigid environment of space. The pair of spacecraft aren’t solar powered: that wouldn’t be possible so far from the Sun. They rely on <cover>radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTG) for their energy. Each of the Voyager probes has three RTGs, and they use plutonium 238 for their fuel source. As that isotope decays, it produces heat which is converted to electrical energy.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"NASA </cover>has Figured Out How to Extend the Lives of the Voyagers Even Longer - Universe Today\" , \"url\": \"https://www.universetoday.com/142802/nasa-has-figured-out-how-to-extend-the-lives-of-the-voyagers-even-longer/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#8_3345698713","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":21,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
