{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":43,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q980"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q980"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#9_757029127","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q980","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#9_757029127\" , \"end_char\": 5120.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Is Plutonium? What Is Plutonium?<cover>What Is Plutonium Used For? Role of Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors</cover>Where Is Plutonium Found on Earth? How Many Neutrons Does Plutonium Have? Management of Recovered Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors Health Hazard Environmental Effects References \" , \"segment\": \"It is formed in nuclear reactors. It is a fissile isotope but can also capture neutrons to form superior isotopes. In the field of nuclear energy, plutionio-239 is used as a nuclear fuel in the production of radioactive isotopes for research and as a fissile agent in nuclear weapons. Plutonium-238 is used in heat sources for space applications, thermoelectric heat generators and has been used in cardiac pacemakers. An important characteristic of many of the isotopes of plutonium is that they exhibit the phenomenon of spontaneous fission, in which the atomic nucleus can be divided spontaneously without the need to be bombarded by any neutron. Management of Recovered Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors In spent fuel, between 7 and 8 "},"time":8},"model_calls":43,"document_judgments":39,"relevance_3":22,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
