{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q980"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q980"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_515048596#3_578491718","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q980","rank":6,"snippet":"V) Neptunium (VI) Neptunium (VII) Hydroxides Oxides Halides Chalcogenides, pnictides, and carbides Chalcogenides Pnictides and carbides Other inorganic Hydrides Phosphates, sulfates, and carbonates Organometallic Coordination complexes Solid state In aqueous solution Applications Precursor in plutonium production Weapons Physics Role in nuclear waste Biological role and precautions References Bibliography Literature External links \" , \"segment\": \"Since then, most neptunium has been and still is produced by neutron irradiation of uranium in nuclear reactors. The vast majority is generated as a by-product in conventional nuclear power <cover>reactors. While neptunium itself has no commercial uses at present, it is used as a precursor for the formation of plutonium-</cover>238, and in radioisotope thermal generators to provide electricity for spacecraft. Neptunium has also been used in detectors of high-energy neutrons . The longest-lived isotope of neptunium, neptunium-237, is a by-product of nuclear reactors and plutonium production. It, and the isotope neptunium-239, are also found in trace amounts in uranium ores due to neutron capture reactions and beta decay. Contents 1 Characteristics 1.1 Physical 1.1.1 Alloys 1.2 Chemical 1.3 Atomic 1.4 Isotopes 1.5 Occurrence 2 History 2.1 Background ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1152658657#0_1303710121","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":11},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
