{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 239 (+ nuclear reactors reactor fuel electricity) (+ energy power fission))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 239 (+ nuclear reactors reactor fuel electricity) (+ energy power fission))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1622416910#1_3408167182","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q981","rank":1,"snippet":"1942 Fermi finally succeeded in producing and controlling a fission chain reaction in… Read More In nuclear weapon: Selecting a weapon design …subcritical piece of uranium-235 (or plutonium-239), would be placed in a gun barrel and fired into the target, another subcritical piece. After the mass was joined (and now supercritical), a neutron source would be used to start the chain reaction. A problem developed with applying the gun method to plutonium,… Read More fissile material In fissile material …percent of naturally occurring uranium), plutonium-239, and uranium-233, the last two being artificially produced from the fertile materials uranium-238 and thorium-232, respectively. A fertile material, not itself capable of undergoing fission with low-energy neutrons, is one that decays into fissile material after neutron absorption within a reactor. Thorium-232 and uranium-238… Read More In uranium processing …latter isotope eventually decays into <cover>plutonium-239—a fissile material of great importance in nuclear power </cover>and nuclear weapons.\" , \"start_char\": 676.000000 , \"title\": \"Plutonium-239 | chemical isotope | Britannica\" , \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/science/plutonium-239\" }"},"time":3268},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":41,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
