{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41918","model_call":49,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q982"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q982"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1256106727#12_2266411838","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q982","rank":5,"snippet":"achieve power flattening across the reactor core. Both Kakrapar-1 and -2 units are loaded with 500kg of thorium fuel to improve their operation at start-up. In 1995, Kakrapar-1 achieved about 300 days of full power operation and Kakrapar-2 about 100 days using thorium fuel. A 30kW mini-reactor has successfully operated at India’s Kamini reactor at Kalpakkam. And the use of thorium-based fuel is planned in Kaiga-1 and -2 and Rajasthan-3 and -4 (Rawatbhata) reactors. India has a three-stage plan for nuclear energy. Stage one uses uranium-fuelled PHWRs (pressurised heavy water reactors) and LWRs to produce plutonium. In stage two this plutonium-based fuel is used in FBRs to breed U-233, and more <cover>plutonium and thorium. Stage three uses advanced heavy water reactors </cover>to burn the U-233 and Pu-239 with thorium. The thorium will produce around 75% of the power, with the spent fuel being reprocessed to recycle fissile materials.\" , \"start_char\": 6476.000000 , \"title\": \"Thorium: Cleaner Nuclear Power?\" , \"url\": \"http://www.power-technology.com/features/feature1141/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1256106727#13_2266413751","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_145214343#8_316451218","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1152658657#0_1303710121","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_515048596#3_578491718","rank":4,"relevance":2}]},"time":6},"model_calls":49,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":25,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
